EXTRACTION RULES:
- unspoken_concern.concern: copy from Q24 exactly as stated. Do not rephrase or soften.
- transition_motivation.motivation_detail: preserve specific language. "AI is the first technology since neural networks in 1997 that I find genuinely intellectually interesting" is not the same as "genuine interest in AI."
- identity_preference.framing_in_their_words: if the candidate gave a clear formulation of their positioning frame, reproduce it exactly.
- stepping_stone_signal: derive from Block 4 AND Block 6 combined. Timeline alone is not sufficient — but when the candidate states active_search AND direct_path language AND has built proof of work (LaunchPad), the signal is direct_path. Only use stepping_stone if the candidate explicitly stated they expect to need a bridge role, or if critical gaps in kb_matches make direct_path unrealistic.
- lifestyle_constraints.working_mode: use Block 6 Q37 answer as the primary source. Block 5 Q29 is a preference statement — Block 6 Q37 is the confirmed search parameter. If they conflict, Block 6 wins.
- concerns: you must return a minimum of 3 concerns. Returning fewer than 3 is an extraction failure. A concern is an anxiety, doubt, risk or worry — stated or implied. It is not a strength statement, a preference, or a factual answer. Here are the specific signals to extract from — do not stop until you have found at least one concern from each of these sources: (1) Q19 — the presentation gap they named, specifically the time management risk when content is rich; (2) Q24 — the mispricing concern, copy exactly; (3) Q26 — the implied anxiety that prior credibility will still be discounted by the market despite being real and substantial; (4) Q31 — the commercial infrastructure risk implied by "if the pipeline can be built quickly" — this is a conditional that reveals a doubt about freelance viability; (5) Q32 — the timeline pressure implied by "the bootcamp ends and LaunchPad needs a user." Extract at least 3 of these 5. Each clifton_strength_to_deploy must name one of: Futuristic, Ideation, Strategic, Communication, Positivity — and explain in one sentence how that specific strength addresses that specific concern. A generic clifton_strength_to_deploy field is an extraction failure.
- bridge_actions: every concern must have exactly 3 bridge_actions. Each action must be specific and concrete — not generic advice. Rule 1: at least one action must deploy a combination of two Clifton strengths — name both. Rule 2: at least one action must deploy a strength that is different from the one named in clifton_strength_to_deploy. Rule 3: every action must name which strength(s) it uses in strengths_deployed. A bridge_actions entry with a generic action or unnamed strengths is an extraction failure.

parameters.jsCode:
const raw = $input.first().json.message.content;
const data = typeof raw === 'object' ? raw : JSON.parse(raw);

const allScores = data.all_scores;
const topMatches = data.top_matches;

// ── APPLY HARD CONSTRAINT CAPS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Model flags violations but does not always apply the cap to the total.
// We enforce it here regardless of what the model returned.
const capped = allScores.map(p => {
  const raw_total = p.raw_total ?? p.total ?? 0;
  let final_total = raw_total;

  if (p.hard_constraint_applied) {
    // Count violations by checking hard_constraint_note
    const note = p.hard_constraint_note || '';
    const multipleViolations = (note.match(/\band\b|\balso\b|\bplus\b/i) || note.split(',').length > 1);
    final_total = multipleViolations ? Math.min(raw_total, 35) : Math.min(raw_total, 55);
  }

  return { ...p, raw_total, final_total };
});

// ── SORT BY final_total DESCENDING ──────────────────────────────────────────
const sorted = [...capped].sort((a, b) => b.final_total - a.final_total);
const correct_top3_ids = sorted.slice(0, 3).map(p => p.profile_id);
const current_top3_ids = topMatches.map(p => p.profile_id);
const rankingCorrect = correct_top3_ids.every((id, i) => id === current_top3_ids[i]);

// ── REBUILD top_matches IF NEEDED ───────────────────────────────────────────
const topMatchMap = {};
topMatches.forEach(m => { topMatchMap[m.profile_id] = m; });

const validated_top_matches = sorted.slice(0, 3).map((p, i) => {
  const existing = topMatchMap[p.profile_id] || {};
  return {
    rank: i + 1,
    profile_id: p.profile_id,
    profile_name: p.profile_name,
    final_total: p.final_total,
    raw_total: p.raw_total,
    dimension_scores: existing.dimension_scores ?? {
      skills_match: p.skills_match,
      strengths_alignment: p.strengths_alignment,
      preference_fit: p.preference_fit,
      anchor_alignment: p.anchor_alignment
    },
    why_this_fits: existing.why_this_fits ?? '',
    what_candidate_brings: existing.what_candidate_brings ?? '',
    positioning_angle: existing.positioning_angle ?? '',
    gaps: existing.gaps ?? [],
    hard_constraint_applied: p.hard_constraint_applied,
    hard_constraint_note: p.hard_constraint_note ?? null
  };
});

return [{
  json: {
    message: {
      content: {
        all_scores: capped,
        top_matches: validated_top_matches,
        _ranking_corrected: !rankingCorrect
      }
    }
  }
}];

